WEBVTT

q0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.190
How do we turn a rule that sounds reasonable into something every machine must answer the same way?

q0002
00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:18.380
This episode has one job: Boolean computation turns a decision rule into a precise assignment-to-output mapping that can be represented and evaluated in several different ways.

q0003
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:27.570
We build on Conceptual animation versus measured result; their definitions stay fixed while this lesson adds a new layer.

q0004
00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:35.381
A Boolean rule maps every complete assignment of its variables to exactly one true-or-false output.

q0005
00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:43.192
Watch for the label Boolean variable; it stays attached to the artifact this episode means.

q0006
00:00:43.192 --> 00:00:50.023
When we say assignment, the highlight identifies its layer before we interpret it.

q0007
00:00:50.023 --> 00:00:58.815
The term Boolean output receives its own visual state so it cannot drift into a neighboring concept.

q0008
00:00:58.815 --> 00:01:07.607
The next sequence uses Four-variable truth layout as a conceptual teaching example, not as measured benchmark evidence.

q0009
00:01:07.607 --> 00:01:11.497
Our stable example is Four-variable truth layout.

q0010
00:01:11.497 --> 00:01:20.687
F(A,B,C,D)=(A AND B) XOR (C OR D), row variables A,B, column variables C,D, MSB-first ordering.

q0011
00:01:20.687 --> 00:01:28.008
Carry assignments, live support, explicit CM, packed truth vectors, and exact output across episodes.

q0012
00:01:28.008 --> 00:01:35.329
We now construct the Four-variable truth layout and name every part before interpreting it.

q0013
00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:44.519
Watch us toggle one input at a time and trace why the rule's output changes or stays fixed.

q0014
00:01:44.519 --> 00:01:53.709
Keep this separation visible: the rule's meaning from any later choice of matrix, graph, solver, or packed evaluator.

q0015
00:01:53.709 --> 00:01:59.560
Now isolate decision rule to assignment-to-output mapping inside Four-variable truth layout.

q0016
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:08.750
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for decision rule to assignment-to-output mapping changes.

q0017
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:17.940
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to decision rule to assignment-to-output mapping, not to a substituted workload.

q0018
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:24.771
The rule expands into assignment cards that become rows of an output column.

q0019
00:02:24.771 --> 00:02:31.602
Now isolate why repeated exact Boolean evaluation is useful inside Four-variable truth layout.

q0020
00:02:31.602 --> 00:02:40.792
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for why repeated exact Boolean evaluation is useful changes.

q0021
00:02:40.792 --> 00:02:49.982
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to why repeated exact Boolean evaluation is useful, not to a substituted workload.

q0022
00:02:49.982 --> 00:02:56.813
A concrete four-input decision rule lights its inputs and one true/false output.

q0023
00:02:56.813 --> 00:03:04.134
A Boolean expression maps each complete assignment of its variables to one Boolean output.

q0024
00:03:04.134 --> 00:03:09.985
Read that statement only within this scope: current Boolean expression implementation.

q0025
00:03:09.985 --> 00:03:13.385
Its declared measurement boundary is semantics.

q0026
00:03:13.385 --> 00:03:16.295
The uncertainty field says none.

q0027
00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:25.485
The nearest confusing lesson is Expression, truth table, and Boolean function; it owns syntax-versus-semantics distinction, while this episode owns decision rule to assignment-to-output mapping.

q0028
00:03:25.485 --> 00:03:34.675
The example fixes F of A, B, C, and D as A AND B, exclusive-or C OR D.

q0029
00:03:34.675 --> 00:03:41.506
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate decision rule to assignment-to-output mapping.

q0030
00:03:41.506 --> 00:03:50.696
A and B select the row, C and D select the column, and MSB-first order fixes both index conventions.

q0031
00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:58.507
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate why repeated exact Boolean evaluation is useful.

q0032
00:03:58.507 --> 00:04:07.697
Every four-bit assignment names exactly one of the sixteen cells, and every cell returns one exact output bit.

q0033
00:04:07.697 --> 00:04:15.508
Folding the truth-table output column into a four-by-four grid changes the layout, not the function.

q0034
00:04:15.508 --> 00:04:24.698
Reading a cell forward evaluates an assignment; reading it backward recovers the row and column bits that selected it.

q0035
00:04:24.698 --> 00:04:33.888
Live support can remove an input from active computation even when the ambient layout still reserves its axis.

q0036
00:04:33.888 --> 00:04:43.078
A packed truth vector stores the same exact outputs in a different physical arrangement from the displayed matrix.

q0037
00:04:43.078 --> 00:04:52.268
The explicit matrix is dense by contract, so compactness and speed require separate evidence rather than definition alone.

q0038
00:04:52.268 --> 00:05:00.079
Holding variable order fixed makes matrix, packed-vector, and program views comparable without an indexing ambiguity.

q0039
00:05:00.079 --> 00:05:09.269
This lesson motivates exact Boolean computation; it does not establish that CM is the best representation for every task.

q0040
00:05:09.269 --> 00:05:17.571
Predict the output after one input changes, then explain which part of the rule caused it.

q0041
00:05:17.571 --> 00:05:24.892
The stable object is the assignment-to-output mapping, not the syntax or storage chosen later.

q0042
00:05:24.892 --> 00:05:34.082
A common mistake is this: Boolean computation is only about one written expression rather than the function over all assignments.

q0043
00:05:34.082 --> 00:05:40.913
Repair it by returning to the owned distinction: decision rule to assignment-to-output mapping.

q0045
00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:53.166
Use the episode's central distinction to answer: The stable object is the assignment-to-output mapping, not the syntax or storage chosen later.

q0046
00:05:53.166 --> 00:05:59.997
First fix the function; only then choose how to represent or evaluate it.
